#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""speakerguardd-selfcheck: prove this Mac's speaker wiring, silently, before
speakerguardd may grant.

    speakerguardd-selfcheck run      --params BLOB [--force] [--defer] [--switch-interface] [-- TUNING-SOURCE...]
    speakerguardd-selfcheck pending
    speakerguardd-selfcheck check    --params BLOB
    speakerguardd-selfcheck verify   --params BLOB [--json FILE] [--attestations FILE] [-- TUNING-SOURCE...]
    speakerguardd-selfcheck generate --pipewire-dir D --wireplumber-dir D

It runs as root, before the supervisor, while the kernel holds the speakers
at its floor, in two units:

- speakerguardd-selfcheck.service, before any login, runs `run --defer`. A
  passing record bound to this board, kernel, blob and wiring (every boot
  without a kernel or tuning change) is reused, and its ExecStartPost puts
  the graph in place before the session starts. The cheap checks (steps 1
  and 2, the card) run here too. Only the silent switch test (step 3), which
  takes about 0.5 s per sense switch, is deferred: the run leaves
  /run/speakerguardd-graph/selfcheck.deferred, writes no record, and no
  graph is generated, so the seat user's session starts with the raw-PCM
  rules alone (provisioning's raw/59-sgv2-raw.conf: no WirePlumber node on
  the raw speaker or sense PCMs, for every user).
- speakerguardd-switchtest.service, after systemd-user-sessions.service and
  before the supervisor, runs `run` only while that file exists (`pending`
  is its ExecCondition). It runs the whole check and writes the record; its
  ExecStartPost generates the graph, and its ExecStopPost (`pending
  --clear`) removes the file, pass or fail. The supervisor requires both
  units, so it grants only after the switch test passed.

While the file exists, no session's PipeWire or WirePlumber starts: their
speakerguardd-graph.service (speakerguardd-provision graph-user, ordered
before both) waits for it to go. WirePlumber's ALSA device probe opens and
prepares every PCM of the speaker card, the raw speaker and sense PCMs
included, whatever its node rules say (the UCM Speaker device and the
pro-audio profile); during the switch test's streams that latched a TDM
clock fault on an amp, and it would take the PCMs the test needs. So the
login is not held, and the session's audio starts once the test is done,
with the graph when it passed.

A board without V/I sense has no switch test (its check is static) and is
never deferred. Runs take an exclusive lock (selfcheck.lock in the state
directory), so the two units never overlap.

1. **Derive** the wiring (wiring.py) from the device tree and the tuning's
   graph (`aurora-speaker-tuning dump --json` on the same source the
   parameters were compiled from), and the rest of the machine description
   (wiring.describe) from the same device tree. Nothing is hand-written per
   board; a board the derivation cannot place fails.
2. **Check the map against the tuning**: every physical channel's amp (its
   device-tree side and role) is the speaker its unit channel models.
3. **Check the map against the hardware**, silently:
   - the ALSA card is the one the device tree's model names, and (with V/I
     sense) the speaker PCM takes the format the transport gives;
   - every amp's registers (regmap): its RX slot is its speaker PCM
     channel, and its V/I sense TX slots are the device tree's;
   - with a zero-sample stream on the speaker PCM (every sample is 0, so
     nothing is audible), it turns each amp's VSENSE and then its ISENSE
     switch off in turn and records the sense PCM: exactly the one sense
     channel the map gives that amp's V (or I) must go quiet or fixed
     (an idle pattern), and every other channel must stay live.
   The kernel admits the stream only under a grant, so the check takes
   "Speaker Protection" itself: one grant, renewed as a 50 ms lease only
   while its own zero-sample stream owns the speaker PCM, for the whole
   switch test (the kernel logs one grant and one release). It keeps the
   PCMs to itself and tells the session helper not to link
   (/run/speakerguardd-selfcheck/active).
4. **Bind** the result to the board, the kernel release, the parameter
   blob's SHA-256 and the wiring hash, in /var/lib/speakerguardd/selfcheck.bind
   (and a readable report beside it). The supervisor grants only with a
   passing record for exactly that combination, and only while the graph
   reports the same wiring. A kernel or tuning change makes the record
   stale, and the next start runs the check again.

Any failure writes a failing record with every reason, exits 1, and leaves
the speakers at the kernel's floor.

`verify` is the whole proof another tool (aurora-speaker-upgrade) asks for
before it switches a Mac to v2. It changes nothing, plays only zero samples,
and stops at the first failing step:

0. **kernel**: the v2 interface, alone or beside the volume lock (the dual
   kernel's "Speaker Protection Interface"); a legacy-only kernel cannot run.
1. **hardware** (steps 1-3 above): a passing record bound to this board,
   kernel and blob. Without one (never run, or stale) it cannot run (2).
2. **voicing** (`aurora-speaker-tuning voicing-check`): the Mac's own chain
   compiled from its own tuning, with no stub, unwired parameter or compiler
   gap; the render invariants on this Mac (finite at the clip edge, silence
   in is silence out, the limiter's saved ceiling holds, deterministic); and
   the macOS parity attestation for this tuning: the gate renders every
   tuning of the macOS release through Apple's own Audio Units and through
   this code (tools/diffrig/aidgate/attest.py), and this tuning must be
   within its tolerance, for this build's rendering code. A tuning with no
   attestation, or one for other code, is refused.
3. **graph**: the speaker graph must hold the 512-frame quantum the gate
   measured (generated config); with a session up, the speaker and sense
   nodes must show no xrun since the graph started beyond one per grant
   (with nothing playing the path is powered down, and the first cycle of a
   sink that starts again may be counted while the plugin still holds the
   wake; no frame is lost there), and a silent pilot (zero-sample streams
   into the voicing input, started and stopped PILOT_STARTS times, after
   one more that wakes the path) must leave their xrun counts unchanged.
   Without a running v2 graph only the generated config is checked.

Exit codes: 0 pass (the switch may proceed), 1 refused (stay on the legacy
stack; reason on stderr), 2 cannot run (retry later; do not switch), 3
internal error (treat as refused). `--json FILE`: {"result", "board",
"kernel", "params_sha256", "wiring_hash", "steps": [{"name", "result",
"detail"}]}.
"""
